{
  "record_id": "034",
  "problem": "Circuit versus effective distance",
  "collection": "Original research investigation",
  "summary": "The premise R≥d is lost in the root statement and its carried-forward caveats. An implication established under that hypothesis does not by itself establish the same implication for every round count.",
  "review_status": "source_reviewed",
  "review_note": "No open interpretation blocker was recorded in the preceding source review. This is not a new independent mathematical verification.",
  "original": {
    "excerpt": "> In graph-like detector error models with fixed boundaries, a proven necessary-and-sufficient\n> structural criterion (in graph-covering, voltage-graph, or chain-complex form) characterizes when\n> d_circ = d_eff, of the shape \"d_circ equals the minimum over nontrivial effective logicals gamma\n> of the length of the shortest closed spacetime lift of gamma\", with zero time-direction\n> voltage/monodromy as the equal-weight liftability condition; the criterion recovers the\n> non-interleaved equality d_circ = d_eff as a corollary and is accompanied by an explicit\n> interleaved example with d_circ > d_eff.",
    "context": "An assembly imported a proved-under-assumptions equality criterion from a child theorem into an unrestricted root statement. The original child theorem explicitly assumes a memory experiment with R≥d rounds.",
    "locator": "Root assembly dated 22 August 2026, root statement, lines 121–127; imported criterion in section 2.1."
  },
  "witness": {
    "explanation": "The premise R≥d is lost in the root statement and its carried-forward caveats. An implication established under that hypothesis does not by itself establish the same implication for every round count.",
    "evidence": "The assembly restates the imported result as: for every legal, measurement-consistent schedule M and R≥d, d_circ(C_R(M))=d_eff(M) iff a minimum effective logical γ has repair(γ)=0. Its root claim instead says the structural criterion characterizes d_circ=d_eff without an R quantifier or restriction. None of the assembly's listed caveats restores R≥d. This is a direct premise-versus-conclusion scope mismatch. The independent audit found no counterexample at R=1 on its d=2 test instance, so the witness invalidates the unrestricted inference, not the theorem for small R.",
    "scope": "argument_invalid"
  },
  "archived_witness_excerpt": "The assembly's own restatement of the certified criterion (§2.1) reads:\n\n> \"For every legal, measurement-consistent single-ancilla CSS schedule `M`, **and `R ≥ d`**,\n> `d_circ(C_R(M)) = d_eff(M) ⟺ ∃γ ∈ L_eff` with `|γ| = d_eff(M)` and `repair(γ) = 0`.\"\n\nI checked the source: iff CONJECTURED 5.7 does carry `R ≥ d`. But:\n\n* the **root claim** has no `R` quantifier and no `C_R(M)` — it says \"characterizes when\n  `d_circ = d_eff`\", unrestricted;\n* the **`lift.iff` child claim** has no `R` quantifier either;\n* §6 of the assembly — the section whose entire job is \"every caveat carried forward, none may be\n  dropped by a promotion of the root\" — lists C-R1..3, C-M1..6, C-S1..3, C-O1..6 and **`R ≥ d` is\n  in none of them**. It appears once, in §2.1, and never again.\n\nThis is exactly the failure mode §0.3 was written to prevent, applied to the assembly itself. I\nprobed for a live counterexample and did not find one at `d = 2`: my enumeration gives\n`d_circ = 2` for the interleaved witness and `1` for its non-interleaved partner at `R = 1` as\nwell as `R ≥ 2`, so `R ≥ d` is not demonstrably load-bearing *on this instance*. That does not\ndischarge it — a hypothesis of the certified theorem is a hypothesis of anything assembled from\nit, and `d_R` is only known to be non-increasing and constant for `R ≥ d`.\n",
  "visible_reasoning": {
    "availability_description": "子节点及组装推理分散提到 MC、R 条件；安装到根定理时的遗漏可由工件看出，但没有完整同一生成节点的推理对照来确定形成阶段。",
